    "redesign_faq_schema": {
      "type": "custom-liquid",
      "name": "Redesign FAQ schema",
      "settings": {
        "custom_liquid": "<script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"FAQPage\", \"mainEntity\": [{\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"What can inherited jewelry become?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"A grandmother's or mother's ring can be reset into a ring that fits your style, or turned into a necklace you'll wear daily. Stones from a brooch or earrings you'd never wear become studs, a pendant, or accents in a new design. A collection of inherited charms and odds and ends can be reimagined as one custom necklace. And where one estate is shared between several heirs, a diamond can become a pendant for a daughter while the band becomes rings for two sons.\"}}, {\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"Can I redesign my grandmother's ring?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Yes — and please don't worry that it's somehow disrespectful. In my experience it's the drawer that ends a story, not the redesign. Jewelry is meant to be worn; she wore hers. When her diamond is on your finger in a setting that's unmistakably you, people ask about it — and you get to tell them about her.\"}}, {\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"How does heirloom redesign work?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Send me a few clear photos of the pieces you've inherited, along with any inspiration images. From there we'll design together, working through style, stones, metals, shape, and scale until we arrive at something that feels exactly right. Everything can be done remotely with insured, tracked shipping, and every piece is handled in my studio by exactly one person: me. There's no cost to begin the conversation.\"}}, {\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"What does heirloom redesign cost?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Usually much less than people expect, because the most valuable parts — the stones and the gold — are already yours. It helps to have a comfortable ballpark budget in mind; I work backward from it. If there's old gold in the mix — even broken chains or single earrings — I refine it and apply the full value toward your new piece.\"}}]}</script>",
        "color_scheme": "",
